These are separate outcomes. Unresolved retained resources or invalid scheduling
prevent claiming full settlement, not saving the session. After inventorying
what is known, continue permitted lesson capture, tool-friction reporting, and
lightweight schedule/unlaned-issue checks through `/wrap-up` or the local
fallback below. This applies with or without issuectl, including clean harvested
work whose authorized native cleanup is blocked and genuinely unmerged work.
Neither case grants deletion authority: leave retained resources untouched
unless the user has authorized a supported native lifecycle action. Do not
control foreign runs or bypass native checks to make the handoff look clean.

Read an available `/wrap-up` contract before using it. If one of its actions
depends on settled resources, a valid schedule, or another missing prerequisite,
skip only that action and disclose why; continue independent preservation.
Record tool friction through the permitted reporting path, not invented tracker
files. When the external skill is absent, use the repository-local fallback;
its absence alone does not block saving the session. Report session preservation,
resource settlement, and schedule verification separately, with unresolved run
ids, failures, skipped actions, and the next authorized recovery action. Never
claim all resources settled or the schedule valid without evidence.

## Narrative root and product root

Some projects keep the handoff in a private narrative root (your cwd) and the
code, the issuectl tracker, and the worker source in a separate public product
root; `/stint-start` describes the split and why it exists. A project is split
only when its narrative-root instructions or handoff, or the user, name the
product root as an absolute path; otherwise both are the same directory and
nothing here changes.

In a split project the handoff, private decision records, and lessons about
the conductor workflow are committed in the narrative root. Everything you
check about the product is read in the product root: landing by content in
product git, the foreign-run list as `run list --repo <product root>`, and the
schedule as `issuectl --root <product> dag --json --reservations <holds>`. An
answer that belongs to a product issue goes there through `issuectl --root
<product>`, since that is where the next round reads it, and is committed in
the product by exact path; nothing private goes into the product.

An owned run may have left private output under
`<narrative root>/worker-results/<full-run-id>/`. Once that run's product
landing is verified, commit its directory by explicit paths, in its own commit
before the handoff commit so the handoff commit stays exactly the handoff
files. This is also how a handoff after an interruption reconciles a run that
landed while its notes were left uncommitted: from the run id in the path, not
by asking for the work again. A directory whose run did not land is preserved
work; leave it uncommitted and untouched, name it with its run id in the
narrative, and report that the narrative root is therefore not clean.
